\title{Tokamak Control & Simulation}
\meta{slug}{simulation}

\p{
  The viewer above renders 3D cutaway vessel and coil geometries, equilibrium flux surfaces, and closed-loop policy trajectories exported from \code{FUSE.jl 1.2.1} (Meneghini et al. [[Men+24]](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.05894)); see the [Overview](index.html) for the \code{ENN} surrogate and multi-region search formulation.
}

\subtree{
  \title{Exported Tokamak Geometries (\code{FUSE.jl})}

  \p{
    For \code{SPARC}, \code{ARC}, \code{ITER}, and \code{DIII-D} (alongside the 25-vertex \code{TCV} vessel and 20 \code{E}/\code{F}/\code{OH} coils), \code{evals/fuse/worker.jl} initializes \code{IMAS.dd()} device models via \code{FUSE.init}, solves Grad–Shafranov equilibria and active coil currents with \code{ActorEquilibrium(:TEQUILA)}, \code{ActorPFdesign}, and \code{ActorPFactive}, and exports vacuum vessel shells, first-wall contours, toroidal-field \code{TF} D-coils, central solenoid \code{CS}/\code{OH} stacks, poloidal-field \code{PF} rings, and last-closed-flux-surface (\code{LCFS}) contours together with #{I_p}, #{B_0}, #{q_{95}}, #{\beta_N}, and #{P_{\mathrm{fus}}}.
  }
}

\subtree{
  \title{TCV Rampdown Policy Search (\code{toka_gym})}

  \p{
    On the open-source EPFL/MIT \code{toka_gym} TCV plasma rampdown benchmark (Wang et al. [[Wan+25]](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-63917-3), Theiler et al. [[The+26]](https://doi.org/10.1088/1741-4326/ae6d12)), a four-layer policy #{\pi_\theta : \mathbb{R}^{11} \to [-1, 1]^8} of hidden width #{256} (#{D = 136{,}712} weights) is searched directly on closed-loop trajectories (#{20} steps #{\times\, 4} substeps) over #{10{,}000} sequential rounds across #{n = 10} common-random-number seeds (#{800{,}000} rollouts across #{8} configurations; initial error \code{58.12 ± 31.39}, exact paired initialization drift \code{0.00}). Multi-region surrogate-free \code{TuRBO-zero} (#{R = 10}, sparse coordinate perturbations of expected block size #{d_p = 13{,}671}) produces identical trajectories across all four cross-region sharing topologies (\code{origin-only}, \code{box-contain}, \code{ellipsoid-ball}, \code{nearest-center}), dropping below on-policy PPO (\code{20.59}) by round #{2} (\code{20.33 ± 6.58}) and reaching \code{1.42 ± 0.03} (\code{1.4192 ± 0.0306}) at round #{250}, \code{1.38 ± 0.04} at round #{1{,}000}, \code{1.36 ± 0.03} at round #{3{,}000}, and \code{1.31 ± 0.02} (\code{1.3112 ± 0.0157}) at round #{10{,}000}.
  }

  \p{
    Single-region \code{TuRBO-ENN} (#{R = 1}, #{K = 10} Epistemic Nearest Neighbors fitted by leave-one-out log-likelihood with UCB acquisition; [baux.enn](docs.html#baux.enn)) trails #{R = 10} at round #{250} (\code{1.58 ± 0.05} \code{hyperbolic} to \code{1.68 ± 0.08} \code{euclidean}; \code{wasserstein} \code{1.68 ± 0.06}, exact sign-flip #{p_{\mathrm{Holm}} = 0.014}) and steadily closes the gap over #{10{,}000} rounds, reaching \code{1.34 ± 0.03} (\code{1.3409 ± 0.0301}, \code{wasserstein}, paired difference \code{+0.03 ± 0.04}, #{p_{\mathrm{Holm}} = 1.00}; \code{1.35 ± 0.02} \code{euclidean}; \code{1.35 ± 0.03} \code{hyperbolic}; \code{1.37 ± 0.03} \code{conformal}).
  }

  \scope{
    \put\transclude/toc{false}
    \put\transclude/numbered{false}
    \put\transclude/heading{false}
    \transclude{baux-0010}
  }
}

\subtree{
  \title{TORAX Transport & Shaping Policy Search}

  \p{
    To study cross-region incumbent sharing over #{10{,}000} rounds on an open-source transport solver, we evaluate a #{93 \to 256 \to 256 \to 256 \to 19} ELU+#{\tanh} policy (#{D = 160{,}531} parameters; policy architecture following Degrave et al. [[Deg+22]](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-021-04301-9), Tracey et al. [[Tra+24]](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.fusengdes.2024.114161), and Pfau et al. [[Pfa+25]](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.00663)) in \code{TORAX 2.0.0} (Citrin et al. [[Cit+24]](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.06718)). Each rollout evolves \code{iterhybrid} geometry (#{R_0 = 6.2\text{ m}}, #{B_0 = 5.3\text{ T}}, #{15} warmup steps followed by #{T = 20} controlled steps) coupled to four 1D radial transport PDEs for #{T_e(\hat{\rho})}, #{T_i(\hat{\rho})}, #{n_e(\hat{\rho})}, and #{\psi(\hat{\rho})} over #{\hat{\rho} \in [0, 1]}, mapping #{93} radial profile and shaping residuals to #{19} normalized plasma-current, heating, current-drive, fueling, and Miller local-equilibrium shaping (\code{kappa}, \code{delta_u}, \code{delta_l}, #{Z_{\mathrm{axis}}}; Miller et al. [[Mil+98]](https://doi.org/10.1063/1.872666)) actuators:
  }

  ##{
    \begin{aligned}
      R(\hat{\rho}, \vartheta) &= R_{\mathrm{geo}}(\hat{\rho}) + \hat{\rho}\,a_{\min}\cos\bigl(\vartheta + \arcsin(\delta(\hat{\rho},\vartheta))\sin\vartheta\bigr), \\[4pt]
      Z(\hat{\rho}, \vartheta) &= Z_{\mathrm{axis}} + \kappa(\hat{\rho})\,\hat{\rho}\,a_{\min}\sin\vartheta.
    \end{aligned}
  }

  \p{
    Following the multi-component reward formulation of Pfau et al. [[Pfa+25]](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.00663) (Table 6, Eq. 31), five normalized tracking and actuator-regularization scores—Miller contour error against the 32-point \code{shape_70166} template, plasma current tracking #{I_p}, shaping-effort margin, actuator bounds compliance, and auxiliary balance—pass through target/bad-threshold sigmoids #{c_m(v)} (#{\zeta = -\ln 19}) and are combined over #{T = 20} steps on #{[0, 100]} via \code{SmoothMax} (#{\alpha = -3.0}):
  }

  ##{
    \begin{aligned}
      c_m(v) &= \operatorname{clip}\left(2\,\sigma\left(\zeta\,\frac{v - g_m}{b_m - g_m}\right),\, 0,\, 1\right), \\[4pt]
      \mathcal{J}(\theta) &= \frac{100}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T} \operatorname{SmoothMax}_{\alpha = -3}\bigl(c_{\mathrm{LCFS},t},\, c_{I_p,t},\, c_{\mathrm{PF},t},\, c_{\mathrm{volt},t},\, c_{\mathrm{OH},t}\bigr), \\[4pt]
      \operatorname{SmoothMax}_{\alpha}(x_1,\dots,x_5) &= \frac{\sum_{m=1}^{5} x_m e^{\alpha x_m}}{\sum_{m=1}^{5} e^{\alpha x_m}}.
    \end{aligned}
  }

  \p{
    Both configurations below run surrogate-free multi-region \code{TuRBO-zero} (\code{acquisition = "random"}, #{R = 10} regions, \code{incumbent-ucb1} routing, sparse coordinate perturbations of expected block size #{d_p = 16{,}053}, #{2} candidates and #{1} arm per round; [baux.regions](docs.html#baux.regions)) across #{n = 10} paired seeds so only cross-region incumbent sharing differs. Entries report sample \code{mean ± SEM} (\code{ddof = 1}); unadjusted two-sided paired #{t_9} #{p}-values are listed at rounds 8,000 and 10,000, Holm–Bonferroni adjustment across the #{6} post-initialization checkpoints gives #{p_{\mathrm{Holm}} = 0.246}, and an on-policy PPO run reaches \code{44.25} at round 10,000.
  }

